From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: sunxi: cpu_comm: fix the CALL argument ABI and the callwq pointer

Three fixes that were live on the board but existed only as a hand edit
inside one build tree, so they vanished the moment a new tree was built
(found the same night as the tvcap node, see 0096).

1. Do not write current->tgid to local_msg + 0x34.

   The payload starts at +0x28 (comp_id); the adapter is handed a pointer
   there and reads its arguments at +4/+8/+12, i.e. msg+0x2c/+0x30/+0x34.
   So +0x34 is *parameter 3*, not a PID field, and the tgid overwrote the
   third argument before the copy loop below could set it. Harmless for two
   arguments, fatal for three -- Wce_GetWindow loses its third output
   pointer. The sender pid is already at +0x1c, set in SendComm2CPUEx.
   Proven against cstenger's Linux capture (commit ec53910); the bogus
   offset came from the arm32 template, which placed the payload one slot
   later. See doku/67-cpu-comm-linux.md, "Argument-ABI".

2. Zero routine_info before use.

   comm_CallWorkAction calls a function pointer taken from routine_info+88
   for incoming MIPS->ARM CALLs (entry_cmd > 4, e.g.
   MipsHalCallback_SignalChange during SetSource). FindRoutineEx never
   fills those bytes, so the pointer was uninitialised stack. That is the
   ROOT CAUSE recorded in doku/72. The memset is the whole fix: with the
   bytes zeroed the pointer is NULL, the call is not made, and the branch
   that acknowledges the MIPS without calling anything is taken -- which is
   the state the module ran in on the bench on 06.09. 0092 then removes the
   dereference entirely and replaces it with cpu_comm_register_callback().

3. Print the RX-CALL trace at the right offsets.

   The old trace read +0x34 as "pid" and then skipped to +0x38/+0x3c, so it
   showed parameters 1, 2, 4, 5 and labelled parameter 3 as a pid. We read
   the MIPS->ARM callbacks off exactly this trace; a measuring instrument
   shifted by one field is worse there than none.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-07 07:30:00.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_proto.c	2026-09-07 07:45:00.000000000 +0200
@@ -907,17 +907,32 @@
 		 */
 		u16 entry_cmd = *(u16 *)(entry_base);
 		{
-			u32 comp_id = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 40);
-			u32 pid     = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 52);
-			u16 param_cnt = *(u16 *)(entry_base + 8);
-			u32 p0 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 44);
-			u32 p1 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 48);
-			u32 p2 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 56);
-			u32 p3 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 60);
+			/*
+			 * Offsets wie im ausgehenden Pfad (CPUComm_CallEx): die
+			 * Nutzlast beginnt bei +0x28 (comp_id), die Argumente folgen
+			 * lückenlos ab +0x2c. Der frühere Druck las +0x34 als "pid"
+			 * und sprang dann auf +0x38/+0x3c weiter -- er zeigte also die
+			 * Parameter 1, 2, 4, 5 und beschriftete Parameter 3 als pid.
+			 *
+			 * Auf genau diesem Druck lesen wir die MIPS->ARM-Callbacks ab
+			 * (MipsHalCallback_*). Ein um ein Feld verschobenes Messgerät
+			 * ist dort schlimmer als gar keins. Die Absender-pid steht
+			 * nicht in der Nutzlast; die Kanal-pid bei +0x10 ist das Feld,
+			 * das den Aufruf überhaupt zuordnet (Schlüssel pid<<4 | chan).
+			 */
+			u32 comp_id   = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 40);	/* +0x28 */
+			u16 dst_cpu   = *(u16 *)(entry_base +  2);	/* +0x02 */
+			u32 chan_pid  = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 16);	/* +0x10 */
+			u16 param_cnt = *(u16 *)(entry_base +  8);	/* +0x08 */
+			u32 p1 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 44);		/* +0x2c Para 1 */
+			u32 p2 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 48);		/* +0x30 Para 2 */
+			u32 p3 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 52);		/* +0x34 Para 3 */
+			u32 p4 = *(u32 *)(entry_base + 56);		/* +0x38 Para 4 */
+
 			pr_info_ratelimited(
-				"cpu_comm: RX-CALL from cpu=%u cmd=0x%x comp_id=0x%08x pid=%u params=%u [%08x %08x %08x %08x]\n",
-				remote_cpu, entry_cmd, comp_id, pid, param_cnt,
-				p0, p1, p2, p3);
+				"cpu_comm: RX-CALL from cpu=%u chan=0x%x comp_id=0x%08x dst_cpu=%u chan_pid=0x%08x params=%u p[1..4]=[%08x %08x %08x %08x]\n",
+				remote_cpu, entry_cmd, comp_id, dst_cpu,
+				chan_pid, param_cnt, p1, p2, p3, p4);
 		}
 		/* DELIVER-USERSPACE-PROTO 2026-05-07: copy CALL to any open
 		 * /dev/cpu_comm fd before kernel-side dispatch. Mainline has
diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_rpc.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_rpc.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_rpc.c	2026-09-07 07:30:00.000000000 +0200
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/cpu_comm/cpu_comm_rpc.c	2026-09-07 07:45:00.000000000 +0200
@@ -547,8 +547,23 @@
 	/* Store component_id in payload area (offset 40 = 0x28) */
 	*(u32 *)(local_msg + 40) = (u32)comp_id;
 
-	/* Store caller's PID (offset 52 = 0x34) */
-	*(u32 *)(local_msg + 52) = (u32)current->tgid;
+	/*
+	 * KEIN tgid nach +0x34.
+	 *
+	 * Die Nutzlast beginnt bei +0x28 (comp_id): der Adapter bekommt einen
+	 * Zeiger dorthin und liest seine Argumente bei +4/+8/+12, also
+	 * msg+0x2c/+0x30/+0x34. +0x34 ist damit **Parameter 3**, nicht ein
+	 * PID-Feld. Ein tgid an dieser Stelle überschreibt das dritte Argument,
+	 * bevor die Kopierschleife unten es setzen kann — für zwei Argumente
+	 * folgenlos, für drei (Wce_GetWindow) genau der dritte Ausgabezeiger.
+	 *
+	 * Die Absender-pid steht ohnehin schon bei +0x1c, gesetzt in
+	 * SendComm2CPUEx (cpu_comm_proto.c, `call_slot + 28`).
+	 *
+	 * Belegt per Linux-Mitschnitt von cstenger (Commit ec53910); die
+	 * Herkunft hier ist die arm32-Vorlage, die die Nutzlast eine Stelle
+	 * später vermutete. Siehe doku/67-cpu-comm-linux.md, „Argument-ABI".
+	 */
 
 	/* Store param count in cmd_type field (offset 8) */
 	*(u16 *)(local_msg + 8) = (u16)param_count;
@@ -931,6 +946,9 @@
 	u8 routine_info[96];
 	u8 *share_seq;
 
+	/* TEST 06.09.: FindRoutineEx fuellt +88..95 nie -- vorher Stack-Muell. */
+	memset(routine_info, 0, sizeof(routine_info));
+
 	if (!work)
 		return;
 
